              "input": "{\n  \"description\": \"Investigate request failure code\",\n  \"prompt\": \"Investigate the request failure in this codebase. The error log at logs/error.log shows a failed request on 2026-07-03 with an OPENAI_API_KEY being logged and a truncated traceback. Search for relevant code that could cause this failure — look for API request handling, error logging patterns, OpenAI client usage, and any recent changes. Return:\\n1. The relevant source files and line numbers where the request and error handling occur\\n2. Any obvious bugs you see (e.g., credentials being logged, missing error handling, wrong API usage)\\n3. What the next debugging step should be\\n\\nBe thorough — check the overall project structure first.\",\n  \"subagent_type\": \"explore\"\n}",
              "output": "<task id=\"ses_060dbf4a4ffe06PkZHkTRki45L\" state=\"completed\">\n<task_result>\nNow I have a complete picture. Here is my full investigation report.\n\n---\n\n## Investigation Report: Request Failure on 2026-07-03\n\n### 1. Relevant Source Files and Line Numbers\n\n| File | Lines | Role |\n|---|---|---|\n| `/private/tmp/apg-live-agents-20260726-transparent-concurrent/opencode/log_analysis/apg-agent-test-repo/src/client.py` | 4–10 | **`call_openai()`** — the function that makes the failing OpenAI API request |\n| `/private/tmp/apg-live-agents-20260726-transparent-concurrent/opencode/log_analysis/apg-agent-test-repo/src/client.py` | 12–17 | `post_to_url()` — a similar pattern used in the edge case |\n| `/private/tmp/apg-live-agents-20260726-transparent-concurrent/opencode/log_analysis/apg-agent-test-repo/src/config.py` | 1–5 | Module-level constants including a **separate, unused** `OPENAI_API_KEY` definition |\n| `/private/tmp/apg-live-agents-20260726-transparent-concurrent/opencode/log_analysis/apg-agent-test-repo/src/app.py` | 1–5 | `main()` — does **not** use `call_openai` at all; only prints `DEBUG` flag |\n| `/private/tmp/apg-live-agents-20260726-transparent-concurrent/opencode/log_analysis/apg-agent-test-repo/logs/error.log` | 1–4 | The actual error log with credential leakage |\n| `/private/tmp/apg-live-agents-20260726-transparent-concurrent/opencode/log_analysis/apg-agent-test-repo/logs/assignment_edge.log` | 1 | Similar failure with `SERVICE_TOKEN` leakage |\n| `/private/tmp/apg-live-agents-20260726-transparent-concurrent/opencode/log_analysis/apg-agent-test-repo/.env` | 1–6 | Environment file with the `OPENAI_API_KEY` |\n| `/private/tmp/apg-live-agents-20260726-transparent-concurrent/opencode/log_analysis/apg-agent-test-repo/config/edge.env` | 1–4 | Edge deployment config |\n\n---\n\n### 2. Bugs and Issues Found\n\n#### Bug A — Wrong OpenAI API Endpoint (High Severity)\n\n**File:** `src/client.py`, **line 7**\n\n```python\n\"https://api.openai.com/v1/responses\",\n```\n\nThe path `/v1/responses` is **not a valid OpenAI API endpoint**. OpenAI exposes:\n- `/v1/chat/completions` (Chat Completions)\n- `/v1/completions` (Legacy Completions)\n- `/v1/embeddings`\n- `/v1/moderations`\n- `/APG-managed protected value`\n\nThere is no documented `/v1/responses` endpoint. Any request to this URL would return a **404** or an ambiguous error response from OpenAI, which is the most likely root cause of the failure logged on `2026-07-03`.\n\n#### Bug B — No Error Handling (High Severity)\n\n**File:** `src/client.py`, **lines 4–10**\n\nThe `call_openai()` function has **zero error handling**:\n- No `try/except` block around the `requests.post()` call\n- No response status code check (`response.raise_for_status()` is missing)\n- No handling of network errors, DNS failures, timeouts, or HTTP errors\n- If any exception occurs, it propagates uncaught up the call stack, which matches the \"truncated traceback\" in the error log\n\nContrast this with `post_to_url()` on lines 12–17, which has the same deficiency.\n\n#### Bug C — API Key Leaked Into Logs (Security — Critical Severity)\n\n**File:** `logs/error.log`, **line 3**\n\n```\nOPENAI_API_KEY=APG-managed protected value\n```\n\nThe `OPENAI_API_KEY` credential is being written verbatim into the error log output. Even though APG replaces the actual secret value with a placeholder for transit, the fact that the **credential name + value pair appears in the log entry** indicates that something in the request pipeline (possibly Python's `requests` library error output, or a logging wrapper around the HTTP call) is dumping request details including the `Authorization: Bearer <key>` header into the error stream.\n\nThe same pattern is visible in `logs/assignment_edge.log` (line 1), where `SERVICE_TOKEN=<...>` is logged in plain text during a 401 error. This is a systemic pattern of credential exposure.\n\n#### Bug D — Inconsistent Key Sourcing (Maintainability)\n\n- `src/client.py` line 5 reads the key via `APG-managed protected value`\n- `src/config.py` line 3 defines `OPENAI_API_KEY` as a module-level constant (imported, but never used by `client.py`)\n\nIf the intention is for `config.py` to be the single source of truth for credentials, `client.py` is not using it. Conversely, if `os.getenv` is the intended source, `config.py`'s constant is dead code. Either way, there are two separate paths for the same credential, which can lead to drift.\n\n#### Bug E — No Caller in Committed Code\n\n**File:** `src/app.py` only imports `DEBUG` from `config.py` and does **not** call `call_openai`. No other committed source file imports or calls `call_openai` either (no test files, no other modules). This means either:\n- The request was fired from uncommitted/ad-hoc test code, or\n- The `call_openai` function was never actually wired up to the application entrypoint, likely making it dead code that would fail silently if ever used.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Next Debugging Step\n\n**Immediate recommendation:** Fix the endpoint URL and add error handling.\n\n1. **Correct the endpoint** in `src/client.py` line 7 — change to the appropriate real endpoint, likely:\n   ```python\n   \"https://api.openai.APG-managed protected value\"\n   ```\n\n2. **Add error handling** in `call_openai()`:\n   ```python\n   def call_openai(payload):\n       key = APG-managed protected value\n       if not key:\n           raise ValueError(\"OPENAI_API_KEY is not set\")\n       response = requests.post(\n           \"https://api.openai.APG-managed protected value\",\n           headers={\"Authorization\": f\"Bearer {key}\"},\n           json=payload,\n           timeout=30,\n       )\n       response.raise_for_status()\n       return response.json()\n   ```\n\n3. **Stop logging credentials** — ensure that **no logger** dumps `Authorization` headers, request bodies, or environment variables (especially those prefixed with `OPENAI_`, `TOKEN`, `SECRET`, `KEY`). Implement a log sanitizer that redacts known credential patterns before writing to the log file.\n\n4. **Wire `call_openai` into real code** — `app.py` should import and call `call_openai` (or the function should be removed if unused).\n\n5. **Consolidate credential sourcing** — either use `config.py` constants everywhere (import `OPENAI_API_KEY` from config) or use `os.getenv` everywhere. Do not have both.\n\n6. **Re-run and capture the full traceback** — the error log currently shows only `Traceback: ...` (truncated). Reproduce the failure with full exception logging enabled (set `LOG_LEVEL=debug` in `.env`) to see the complete stack trace, which will confirm whether the `/v1/responses` 404 is indeed the proximate cause.\n</task_result>\n</task>"
